algorithm.detail:
Tabulate every message $A \to B : \text{fields}, Sig_{sk_A}(\text{fields})$:
\begin{tightitem}
\item who sends what, and which key protects it;
\item exactly which fields are inside the signature/MAC versus sent bare;
\item the stated goals (only Alice authorizes, the verifier checks the originator requested it, \ldots);
\item the attacker model (outsider vs malicious participant vs MitM).
\end{tightitem}

Verify every security-relevant field lies inside the signed/MAC'd blob:
\begin{tightitem}
\item identities of sender and recipient;
\item amounts, exercise ids, producer names;
\item $Hash(File)$ for any bulk data.
\end{tightitem}
Any field left outside can be substituted: signing only $Amount$ lets an attacker reuse $Sig_{sk_{Alice}}(Amount)$ to pay a different merchant; a release signature without $Hash(File)$ lets the attacker swap the bulk content after signing. Fix: move the missing field (or the hash of the bulk data) inside the signed portion.

For each sub-question, answer 'possible' or 'not possible' first.
\begin{tightitem}
\item Not possible $\Rightarrow$ name the blocking mechanism (e.g. the Bank verifies $Sig_{sk_{Alice}}(M)$ against $M$ and $pk_{Alice}$, so modifying $Amount$ breaks verification).
\item Possible $\Rightarrow$ give the concrete attack sequence, then a minimal fix, stating which goal the fix restores.
\end{tightitem}
\imp{Never propose a fix without naming the flaw it addresses.}
